# Peers

Public repos worth tailing for Effect, Alchemy, and XState patterns. Discovery uses `pnpm find-peers` and the [find-peers skill](https://github.com/joelhooks/rat-stack/blob/main/skills/find-peers/SKILL.md). Shared lines do not mean identical versions. Drift is expected.

S means an upstream reference or an adopted pattern; A means active, close to our stack, and studied; B means relevant and worth tailing. C means screened without a relevant pattern for current work; D means off-stack; E means stale without a useful reference; F means no usable reference remains. The skill defines the full rubric. Rank is usefulness, not popularity or version parity.

The full dataset lives in [peers.json](https://github.com/joelhooks/rat-stack/blob/main/.brain/data/peers.json). The content build filters and sorts those rows; changing a tier never deletes a peer. The main roster shows S through B. C through F stay in **Also seen**.

## Our pins

Read from rat-stack's root, infra, and core manifests at build time. `=` means a peer's recorded version matches our current pin; `≠` means it differs. We do not infer compatibility from either marker.

<PeerPins />

Each peer has a checked date and a default-branch commit linked below. `—` means no direct declaration in the inspected project manifests, not proof of no transitive dependency. Catalogs resolve to their declared versions; ranges are labeled and lockfile resolutions are shown where available. Vendored source mirrors and historical test fixtures do not count as current project pins. No peer in this snapshot directly declares `@xstate/effect`.

Refresh peers, versions, and tiers every week or two and after every shared-line bump. The repo-native gardener pass owns the refresh. Update the dataset, not table rows in this page.

